  "query_preview": "../Tuesday through Friday/...\nTuesday will begin with moderate to heavy rainfall and gusty \nsoutherly winds across northern panhandle with an occluded front \npushing ashore with a deeper feed of moisture. Throughout the day,\na rapidly deepening surface low will lift northeast into the \nnorthern Gulf, with a quick moving cold front traversing the Gulf \nand through the panhandle, clearing the southern panhandle on \nWednesday morning. Along and ahead of the front, an atmospheric \nriver will tap into a deeper feed of subtropical moisture, with \nheavy rainfall and strong winds feeding into the panhandle, with \nhighest rain amounts and strongest winds across the northeast Gulf\ncoast and northern panhandle. South to southeasterly winds will \nintensify, producing gale-force conditions in inner channels such \nas northern Lynn Canal and Clarence Strait, with widespread gales \n(34-47 kt) to storm-force winds (48\u201363 kts) and combined seas \nexceeding 20 feet along outer coastal waters, mainly from Cape \nEdgecombe northward.\n\nAs the primary low stalls and weakens in the northern Gulf \nthrough the end of the week, onshore flow and subtle troughs \nswinging around the low will keep higher precipitation chances \nwith moderate to heavy showers and gusty winds across SEAK through\nThursday. Quite a bit of model discrepancy by the end of the \nweek, but general trend is for the low to weaken further as it \nshifts southeast, with a ridge of high pressure building in its \nwake, bringing clearing skies and drier offshore flow.\n\nPersistent onshore flow with mainly cloudy skies and rain will \nkeep temperatures cool and consistent through the period, with \ndaytime highs in the 40s (low 50s possible on Tuesday) and \novernight lows in the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nSnow levels may initially be low enough for snow or rain/snow mix\nnear White Pass early Tuesday morning, but will quickly rise \nthroughout the day as southerly winds pick up ahead of the front. \nSnow levels will fall down to 1-2kft behind the front Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday and snow showers will be possible along the \nKlondike Highway and the upper reaches of the Haines Highway \nthrough Thursday, although any accumulations look to be light for \nnow.",
